 "result": "\n\nScenario `G4-SLK-01` saved.\n\n**Request:** \"Invite the human Rosa Delgado in the #launch-week channel, the one with a @northwind.example address, to the #platform-launch channel.\" — the action is a `conversations.invite` of the identified user (both facts are `users` attributes, so the acted-on record is a user).\n\n**Decoys:**\n- **A:User.email / F8** — a human named Rosa Delgado in #launch-week whose address is `rosa.delgado@northwind.co`: an almost-identical email that fails only the domain check (fails c3).\n- **A:User.email / F1** — a human named Rosa Delgado whose Slack handle is exactly `rosa.delgado` (the requested identity on the username field, the email's designated substitute) but whose address is `rosa.delgado@gmail.com` (fails c3).\n- **A:User.is_bot / F0** — a bot account named Rosa Delgado with a `@northwind.example` address in #launch-week, passing every condition except the \"human\" one (fails c2).\n\nKey design choice: the email condition is a domain match (\"a @northwind.example address\") rather than a full address — since email is unique per user, no second user could pass a full-email condition, which would have made an is_bot decoy impossible. The #launch-week membership condition also guarantees the six pre-seeded people (including the default Priya Sharma) fail at least two conditions, and \"Rosa Delgado\" was chosen to avoid name collisions with them.",
